{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_012yT6HzTWxUfnP6mhDYfYWz", "type": "tool_result", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "{\"excerpt_removed\": true, \"references\": [{\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiseul\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.Wikipedia.com/wiki/Jiseul\"}, {\"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeju_language\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.koreanclass101.com/lesson/korean-teachers-answer-your-questions-5-how-many-dialects-of-korean-are-there?lp=245\"}, {\"url\": \"https://www.koreanclass101.com/lesson/korean-teachers-answer-your-questions-5-how-many-dialects-of-korean-are-there\"}, {\"url\": \"https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=131\"}, {\"url\": \"https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=267\"}, {\"url\": \"https://dspace.cuni.cz/handle/20.500.11956/180279\"}, {\"url\": \"https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=221\"}, {\"url\": \"https://mslib.jejunu.ac.kr/handle/2020.oak/18279\"}]}"}]}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-20T23:49:58.666Z", "tool_use_result": {"query": "Jiseul Jeju dialect for potato", "results": [{"tool_use_id": "srvtoolu_01NsojaXRenetP2a9r6r7WrW", "content": [{"title": "Jiseul", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiseul"}, {"title": "www.wikipedia.com", "url": "https://www.Wikipedia.com/wiki/Jiseul"}, {"title": "Jeju language", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jeju_language"}, {"title": "Korean Teachers Answer Your Questions - Lesson #5 - How Many Varieties of Korean Are There? |", "url": "https://www.koreanclass101.com/lesson/korean-teachers-answer-your-questions-5-how-many-dialects-of-korean-are-there?lp=245"}, {"title": "korean teachers answer your questions 5 how many dialects of korean are there", "url": "https://www.koreanclass101.com/lesson/korean-teachers-answer-your-questions-5-how-many-dialects-of-korean-are-there"}, {"title": "Search: entire dictionary Jeju-eo headwords English headwords Jeju-eo pronunciation guide", "url": "https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=131"}, {"title": "Search: entire dictionary Jeju-eo headwords English headwords Jeju-eo pronunciation guide", "url": "https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=267"}, {"title": "dspace.cuni.cz", "url": "https://dspace.cuni.cz/handle/20.500.11956/180279"}, {"title": "Search: entire dictionary Jeju-eo headwords English headwords Jeju-eo pronunciation guide", "url": "https://talkingdictionary.swarthmore.edu/jeju/?entry=221"}, {"title": "제주대학교 Repository", "url": "https://mslib.jejunu.ac.kr/handle/2020.oak/18279"}]}, "The search indicates that \"jiseul\" is the Jeju word for potato. The 2012 film *Jiseul* takes its title from that meaning, and its entire cast is composed of local actors speaking their natural dialect.\n\nA Korean language lesson gives the same meaning. When asked what \"jisil\" means, a speaker answers that it is potato in the Jeju dialect. The spelling varies across sources (지슬, 지실).\n\nThe Jeju dialect's ordinary word for potato is 감자 (gamja), per a Swarthmore talking dictionary entry, which lists noun potato (Korean) 감자. The same dictionary lists sweet potato as 고구마. I didn't find a separate Jeju term for potato in these results.\n\nContext: It is declining in usage and was classified by UNESCO in 2010 as critically endangered. Some scholars treat it as a separate language rather than a dialect, which is why sources describe it differently."], "durationSeconds": 4.9816941250000015, "searchCount": 1}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_012yT6HzTWxUfnP6mhDYfYWz", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "hook", "reason_type": "hook"}}]}
